> what Flutter is ACTUALLY doing under the hood.

Once you understand rebuild mechanics,
`read`, `watch`, `Consumer`, and `Selector` become obvious.

Right now you’re guessing.
We’re going to fix that properly.

---

# FIRST — THE CORE THING YOU MUST UNDERSTAND

Flutter UI is just:

```text
functions rebuilding widgets
```

That’s it.

Every state management solution exists to answer ONE question:

> WHICH widgets should rebuild when data changes?

That’s the entire game.

---

# Imagine This

You have:

```text
AddExpenseScreen
├── AppBar
├── AmountField
├── CategoryWrap
├── DateSelector
├── PaymentDropdown
└── SaveButton
```

Now user changes category.

Question:

> Should the WHOLE screen rebuild?

NO.

Only:

```text
CategoryWrap
```

needs rebuild.

This is where:

* watch
* read
* Consumer
* Selector

matter.

---

# UNDERSTANDING `watch()`

---

# What `watch()` Means

```dart
context.watch<MyProvider>()
```

means:

```text
"Hey Flutter,
this widget depends on this provider.
If provider changes,
rebuild this widget."
```

That’s all.

---

# Example

```dart
final theme =
    context.watch<ThemeProvider>();
```

Now when:

```dart
notifyListeners()
```

runs,
THIS WIDGET rebuilds.

---

# WHEN TO USE `watch()`

Use `watch()` ONLY when:

> UI must automatically update when state changes.

---

# Example

```dart
Text(
  context.watch<CartProvider>()
      .total
      .toString(),
)
```

If total changes:
Text must update.

Correct use of `watch()`.

---

# BAD USE OF WATCH

Inside button:

```dart
onPressed: () {
  context.watch<CartProvider>()
}
```

WRONG.

Why?

Buttons are actions.
Not rebuilding UI.

---

# UNDERSTANDING `read()`

---

# What `read()` Means

```dart
context.read<MyProvider>()
```

means:

```text
"Give me provider once.
I do NOT care about rebuilds."
```

No listening.
No rebuild subscription.

---

# WHEN TO USE `read()`

Use `read()` for:

* button presses
* form submit
* navigation
* API calls
* validation
* provider methods
* one-time access

---

# Example

```dart
onPressed: () {
  context.read<CartProvider>()
      .checkout();
}
```

Perfect.

Button does NOT need rebuild.

---

# GOLDEN RULE

---

# `watch()`

UI DISPLAY

---

# `read()`

UI ACTIONS

---

# SIMPLE MEMORY TRICK

---

# WATCH

```text
I am LOOKING at state.
```

---

# READ

```text
I just NEED state once.
```

---

# REAL EXAMPLES

---

# GOOD WATCH

```dart
Text(
  context.watch<AuthProvider>()
      .username,
)
```

Username changes → Text rebuilds.

---

# GOOD READ

```dart
onPressed: () {
  context.read<AuthProvider>()
      .logout();
}
```

Button doesn’t need rebuild.

---

# BAD WATCH

```dart
bool valid =
    context.watch<FormProvider>()
```

inside validation method.

No UI rebuild needed.

Use `read()`.

---

# UNDERSTANDING `Consumer`

Now the important optimization part.

---

# Problem With `watch()`

Imagine this:

```dart
Widget build(BuildContext context) {

  final provider =
      context.watch<MyProvider>();

  return Scaffold(
    appBar: AppBar(),
    body: HugeComplicatedWidget(),
  );
}
```

Now:
ANY provider change rebuilds:

* Scaffold
* AppBar
* everything.

Wasteful.

---

# Consumer Fixes This

---

# What Consumer Means

```dart
Consumer<MyProvider>(
  builder: ...
)
```

means:

```text
"ONLY rebuild THIS small section."
```

---

# Example

```dart
Consumer<CartProvider>(
  builder: (context, provider, child) {
    return Text(
      provider.total.toString(),
    );
  },
)
```

Now ONLY Text rebuilds.

Huge optimization.

---

# WHEN TO USE CONSUMER

Use Consumer when:

* only small widget part needs updates,
* whole screen rebuild is unnecessary.

---

# REAL EXAMPLE

---

# BAD

```dart
class HomeScreen extends StatelessWidget {

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {

    final auth =
        context.watch<AuthProvider>();

    return Scaffold(
      appBar: AppBar(
        title: Text(auth.username),
      ),
      body: HugeList(),
    );
  }
}
```

Changing username rebuilds:
ENTIRE screen.

---

# BETTER

```dart
AppBar(
  title: Consumer<AuthProvider>(
    builder: (context, auth, child) {
      return Text(auth.username);
    },
  ),
)
```

Now ONLY title rebuilds.

---

# UNDERSTANDING `Selector`

This is advanced optimization.

---

# Problem With Consumer

Consumer rebuilds when:
ANYTHING in provider changes.

Example:

```dart
class UserProvider {

  String username;
  String email;
  bool isPremium;
}
```

Even if ONLY email changes:
Consumer rebuilds entire widget.

---

# Selector Fixes This

Selector listens ONLY to specific field.

---

# Example

```dart
Selector<UserProvider, String>(
  selector: (_, provider) =>
      provider.username,

  builder: (_, username, __) {
    return Text(username);
  },
)
```

Now widget rebuilds ONLY if:

```text
username changes
```

NOT:

* email
* premium
* anything else.

---

# WHEN TO USE SELECTOR

Use Selector when:

* provider is large,
* many unrelated fields exist,
* rebuild optimization matters.

---

# SIMPLE COMPARISON

---

# read()

```text
Get provider once.
No rebuild.
```

Used for:

* actions.

---

# watch()

```text
Listen and rebuild widget.
```

Used for:

* displaying state.

---

# Consumer

```text
Rebuild ONLY small widget area.
```

Used for:

* optimization.

---

# Selector

```text
Rebuild ONLY if specific value changes.
```

Used for:

* advanced optimization.

---

# REAL SPENDLY EXAMPLES

---

# CATEGORY WRAP

Correct:

```dart
final provider =
    context.watch<CategoryProvider>();
```

Because UI selection changes visually.

---

# SAVE BUTTON

Correct:

```dart
context.read<ExpenseProvider>()
```

Because button only performs action.

---

# DATE TEXT

Best:

```dart
Consumer<DatetimeProvider>(
  builder: ...
)
```

Only date text rebuilds.

---

# PAYMENT METHOD

Eventually:

```dart
Selector<PaymentProvider, String?>(
```

to rebuild ONLY when selectedMethod changes.

---

# HUGE MISTAKE BEGINNERS MAKE

They do:

```dart
final provider =
    context.watch<MyProvider>();
```

at top of EVERY screen.

Now entire app rebuilds constantly.

Performance disaster.

---

# HOW PROFESSIONALS THINK

Professionals ask:

> what EXACTLY needs rebuild?

Not:

> how do I access provider?

That mindset difference matters massively.

